Input: {"question": "In which significant event was trial of Bessie Broun involved?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 95, "text": "\"The Shadow of Suspicion: Unveiling the Trial of Bessie Broun and the Commission of Justiciary\""}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 97, "end": 204, "text": "In the misty dawn of 17th century Scotland, a sense of unease settled over the rural town of Kirkcudbright."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 205, "end": 313, "text": "Whispers of witchcraft and devilry spread like wildfire, fueled by the fervor of the Protestant Reformation."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 314, "end": 483, "text": "Amidst this climate of fear and superstition, a humble woman named Bessie Broun found herself at the center of a maelstrom, accused of consorting with the devil himself."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 484, "end": 719, "text": "The trial of Bessie Broun, a pivotal event in Scotland's tumultuous history, was inextricably linked to the Commission of Justiciary, a method of law enforcement employed by the Scottish authorities to investigate and prosecute crimes."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 720, "end": 862, "text": "This narrative delves into the intricate web of evidence that binds these two entities, shedding light on the dark corners of Scotland's past."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 863, "end": 960, "text": "In the spring of 1654, Bessie Broun, a widow and healer, was arrested on suspicion of witchcraft."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 961, "end": 1111, "text": "The accusations against her were manifold: she was said to have cursed her neighbors' livestock, conjured storms, and even made a pact with the devil."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1112, "end": 1226, "text": "As the trial unfolded, it became clear that the Commission of Justiciary played a crucial role in the proceedings."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1227, "end": 1430, "text": "The Commission, established in 1597, was a powerful instrument of justice, granting the Scottish Privy Council the authority to investigate and try cases of treason, witchcraft, and other serious crimes."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1431, "end": 1620, "text": "Comprised of esteemed judges, lawyers, and nobles, the Commission was tasked with maintaining order and upholding the law in a country torn apart by religious strife and political upheaval."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1621, "end": 1758, "text": "In Bessie's case, the Commission of Justiciary was instrumental in gathering evidence, interrogating witnesses, and overseeing the trial."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1759, "end": 1945, "text": "The Commission's influence was evident in the appointment of the trial's presiding judge, Lord Advocate Sir John Fletcher, a seasoned jurist with a reputation for fairness and integrity."}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1946, "end": 2040, "text": "As the trial progressed, the prosecution presented a damning array of evidence against Bessie."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2041, "end": 2203, "text": "Witnesses testified to her alleged supernatural abilities, and \"spectral evidence\" – testimony about dreams and visions – was used to corroborate the accusations."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2204, "end": 2435, "text": "The Commission's role in validating this evidence cannot be overstated; its members carefully scrutinized the testimony, ensuring that the prosecution's case was built on a foundation of credible witnesses and convincing testimony."}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 187, "text": "As the sun dipped below the rolling hills of the Scottish countryside, the villagers of Kirkcaldy gathered in the town square, their whispers and murmurs carrying on the crisp autumn air."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 188, "end": 256, "text": "The year was 1662, and the witch hysteria had reached a fever pitch."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 257, "end": 370, "text": "Today, they would bear witness to the trial of Bessie Broun, a local healer accused of consorting with the devil."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 371, "end": 515, "text": "Bessie, a wispy-haired woman with eyes that seemed to hold a deep wisdom, stood tall in the center of the square, her hands bound by rough rope."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 516, "end": 603, "text": "The villagers parted as the prosecutor, a stern-faced man named Thomas, approached her."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 604, "end": 681, "text": "He held a parchment in his hand, adorned with the wax seal of the local lord."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 682, "end": 807, "text": "\"Bessie Broun, thou art accused of witchcraft and sorcery,\" Thomas declared, his voice echoing off the surrounding buildings."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 808, "end": 830, "text": "\"How doth thou plead?\""}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 831, "end": 923, "text": "Bessie's gaze swept the crowd, her eyes lingering on the faces of her friends and neighbors."}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 924, "end": 1082, "text": "She knew that many of them had benefited from her healing remedies, her knowledge of the natural world passed down through generations of women in her family."}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1083, "end": 1136, "text": "Yet, in this moment, she saw only fear and suspicion."}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1137, "end": 1185, "text": "\"I do not plead,\" Bessie said, her voice steady."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1186, "end": 1213, "text": "\"For I have done no wrong.\""}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1214, "end": 1229, "text": "Thomas snorted."}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1230, "end": 1297, "text": "\"We have witnesses who will testify to thy dealings with the devil."}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1298, "end": 1444, "text": "Thou hast been seen gathering herbs in the dead of night, and thy touch hath brought strange and wondrous cures to those who hath sought thy aid.\""}, "D2S17": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1445, "end": 1481, "text": "Bessie's eyes flashed with defiance."}, "D2S18": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1482, "end": 1526, "text": "\"I have done nothing but help those in need."}, "D2S19": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1527, "end": 1566, "text": "And I have done so with their consent.\""}, "D2S20": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1567, "end": 1670, "text": "The villagers murmured among themselves, some nodding in agreement, others crossing themselves in fear."}, "D2S21": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1671, "end": 1694, "text": "Thomas's eyes narrowed."}, "D2S22": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1695, "end": 1705, "text": "\"Consent?\""}, "D2S23": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1706, "end": 1718, "text": "he repeated."}, "D2S24": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1719, "end": 1756, "text": "\"What dost thou mean by that, witch?\""}, "D2S25": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1757, "end": 1792, "text": "Bessie's gaze locked onto Thomas's."}, "D2S26": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1793, "end": 1907, "text": "\"I mean that those who have sought my aid have done so willingly, with full knowledge of the remedies I would use."}, "D2S27": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1908, "end": 1968, "text": "They have given their consent, freely and without coercion.\""}, "D2S28": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1969, "end": 2058, "text": "The villagers began to whisper among themselves, some looking at each other in confusion."}, "D2S29": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2059, "end": 2116, "text": "Had they not, too, sought Bessie's help in times of need?"}, "D2S30": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2117, "end": 2205, "text": "Had they not given their consent, implicit or explicit, when they accepted her remedies?"}, "D2S31": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2206, "end": 2257, "text": "As the trial continued, the villagers' doubts grew."}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "Commission of Justiciary", "card_ids": ["d1:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "d1", "card_id": "d1:c0", "entity": "Commission of Justiciary", "scope": "Significant event", "claim": "The trial of Bessie Broun was inextricably linked to the Commission of Justiciary.", "answer": "Commission of Justiciary", "quote": "The trial of Bessie Broun, a pivotal event in Scotland's tumultuous history, was inextricably linked to the Commission of Justiciary, a method of law enforcement employed by the Scottish authorities to investigate and prosecute crimes. This narrative delves into the intricate web of evidence that binds these two entities, shedding light on the dark corners of Scotland's past. In the spring of 1654, Bessie Broun, a widow and healer, was arrested on suspicion of witchcraft. The accusations against her were manifold: she was said to have cursed her neighbors' livestock, conjured storms, and even made a pact with the devil. As the trial unfolded, it became clear that the Commission of Justiciary played a crucial role in the proceedings. The Commission, established in 1597, was a powerful instrument of justice, granting the Scottish Privy Council the authority to investigate and try cases of treason, witchcraft, and other serious crimes. Comprised of esteemed judges, lawyers, and nobles, the Commission was tasked with maintaining order and upholding the law in a country torn apart by religious strife and political upheaval. In Bessie's case, the Commission of Justiciary was instrumental in gathering evidence, interrogating witnesses, and overseeing the trial.", "original_document_number": 1}], "initial_answers": ["Commission of Justiciary"]}
